# Diagram C — the DESIRED TARGET machine for the managed design run.
#
# Third of a triptych. Read the other two first if you have not:
#   workflow-as-prose.d2   (A) the machine the CURRENT SKILL PROSE claims
#   surface-as-built.d2    (B) the deterministic surface AS BUILT — note that B
#                              predates PHASE-16 and says "writer acts — six";
#                              there are now SEVEN (`discharge`), and B's gap g1
#                              ("exploring has NO fragment of its own") is closed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THIS DIAGRAM IS FOR
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SL-233 PHASE-08 rewrites `plugins/doctrine/skills/design/SKILL.md` (214 lines,
# 10,178 bytes) from an eight-state workflow machine into a thin
# activation/recovery adapter. The question this diagram exists to answer is
# narrow and practical: WHAT NEEDS TO CHANGE ABOUT THE SKILL.
#
# It is therefore NOT an attempt to make the design process formally rigorous.
# The incumbent skill was never modelled as an FSM; it was written to stop agents
# forgetting to do things, and neither A nor B would pass muster as a state
# chart. This diagram borrows FSM questions only where they earn their keep —
# is this a state or a transition? what guards a transition? is it one machine or
# several? — and stops at the point where the answer stops changing the skill.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE MEDIATION AXIS — the whole point of the colours
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every obligation in the design process sits in exactly one of three tiers.
# The rule separating the first two is NOT invented here; it is authored in the
# shipped runbook, `install/design-prompts/exploring.toml:8-13`:
#
#     THE AUTHORING RULE. Could a project legitimately do this differently?
#       Yes -> a runbook step. Overridable, verifier substitutable.
#       No  -> an engine invariant, enforced by `apply` / `advance`.
#
# DEC-102 (craft is overridable, invariants stay sealed) states the same line on
# the asset-policy axis: an asset is sealed when a project override would make
# its content FALSE rather than merely different.
#
#   TIER 1  ENGINE INVARIANT   Rust-owned, closed vocabulary, cannot be
#                              overridden or forgotten. `Condition`,
#                              `Refusal`, the CAS revision check.
#   TIER 2  RUNBOOK STEP       Doctrine-sequenced obligation, contents are asset
#                              data (TOML), optionally verified by an executable.
#                              The "do this now" register. DEC-101.
#   TIER 3  SKILL PROSE        Soft. Voluntary. Agent judgement. What is left
#                              after tiers 1 and 2 — and the smaller this tier
#                              gets, the more honest `VA-4`'s size comparison is.
#
# The third tier is the residue, and PHASE-08's real deliverable is deciding how
# much residue is legitimate rather than merely unmoved.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS ALREADY SETTLED BY EVIDENCE (decisions, not proposals)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D1  EIGHT PROSE STATES MAP ONTO FIVE ENGINE STAGES. `Stage` is five coarse
#     landmarks and `gate::can_advance` is the sole authority
#     (src/design_run/gate.rs). The mapping is in the `mapping` panel below.
#     Three of the incumbent's eight "states" are not states at all.
#
# D2  "5 · Write design.md" IS AN EFFECT, NOT A STATE. `materialise` is a CLI
#     verb and `Condition::MaterialisationCurrent` guards drafting → reviewing
#     (gate.rs:174-177). Writing the doc is already an engine-checked effect of
#     being in Drafting. It needs no state and no step.
#
# D3  "7 · Integrate review feedback" IS WORK INSIDE Reviewing, NOT A STATE.
#     This is why the incumbent's own XML has SEVEN states while its numbered
#     list has EIGHT (SKILL.md:19-28 vs :30-57) — the XML already folded it into
#     Adversarial review's outbound transitions. The engine agrees: it is gated
#     at the exit by `IntegratedReviewPresent` + `BlockingFindingsDisposed`
#     (gate.rs:179-184). The discrepancy is a defect in the incumbent, and this
#     diagram resolves it in the XML's favour.
#
# D4  IT IS NOT ONE MACHINE. DEC-065 records separate state models, and B's
#     `runstate.models` shows them: inquiry lifecycle, traversal cursor+posture,
#     attestation/review, delegation, recovery. They are ORTHOGONAL REGIONS, not
#     sub-states of the stage machine. The incumbent skill prose flattens all of
#     them into one numbered list, which is a large part of why it reads as an
#     eight-state machine that does not behave like one.
#
# D5  GUARDS BELONG ON EDGES, AND EVERY NON-TERMINAL STAGE HAS EXACTLY ONE
#     FORWARD EDGE. `can_advance` is total on non-terminal stages, so edge-keying
#     and origin-state-keying are isomorphic. There are exactly FOUR forward
#     edges, and they are the only places an obligation can be hung. This is the
#     structural fact that makes the whole disposition tractable: an obligation
#     either lands on one of four edges, or it stays prose.
#
#     EVIDENCE, CORRECTED. An earlier revision cited gate.rs:186-204 — which is
#     `boundary_runbook`'s DOC COMMENT ASSERTING the isomorphism, i.e. the claim
#     cited as evidence for itself. The proof is:
#       gate.rs:150-158   `can_advance`, a nine-line const fn over a literal
#                         four-arm `matches!`. Read it; the claim is settled.
#       tests.rs:56-77    `stage_gate_table_admits_only_legal_forward_moves`
#                         asserts the admitted set EXHAUSTIVELY over all 25
#                         ordered pairs. A fifth edge cannot land silently.
#     And the linearity has THREE dependants, not one: boundary_runbook
#     (gate.rs:199-204), forward_runbook (commands/design.rs:1801-1806, which
#     WALKS Stage::ALL to find the single edge), and cumulative_conditions
#     (gate.rs:208-218, which iterates Stage::ALL.windows(2) and so assumes a
#     chain). D5 is therefore not an assumption this diagram makes; it is a
#     property the engine holds and a test locks. The open question is DURABILITY
#     — is a branch ever wanted? — and it is item 5 for the reviewer, not item 1.
#
# D6  `set` MODE IS DEFERRED — IMP-373. `Mode` is a one-variant enum
#     (runbook.rs:201-206); `mode = "set"` is a hard parse refusal with a test
#     locking it (:986-993). It collides with `EX-14`'s token bound: only the
#     step AT THE CURSOR carries its full text, and a coverage set has no cursor
#     (notes.md:425-427). Consequence for THIS diagram: any obligation proposed
#     as a runbook here must be admissible as a SEQUENCE, or it stays prose.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FOUR OPEN QUESTIONS — ALL RULED ON BY THE OWNER, 2026-08-01
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D7  (was Q2) THE TWO CHECKLISTS TAKE EDGES 3 AND 4, AS SEQUENCES.
#     The fifteen content items guard drafting → reviewing; the attack list
#     guards reviewing → locked. Both are coverage sets, but they ship as
#     `sequence` and the arbitrary order is accepted: the purpose is to stop
#     agents forgetting, and an imposed order on a short list costs almost
#     nothing. CONSEQUENCE: IMP-373 does NOT block PHASE-08. `set` mode becomes
#     a later refinement, not a precondition. This overrides D6's caution.
#
