# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D13 — THE RULING THAT ARRIVED AFTER THE REST AND RESHAPED THEM (DEC-103)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D13 INSTRUCTION IS DELIVERED AT THE POINT OF EFFECT. Ruled 2026-08-01, after
#     D1-D12, in response to a self-check pass that found two of D11's four
#     deletions dropped an obligation at the moment it fires. Recorded as
#     DEC-103.
#
#       Instruction — and guardrails governing invariants the system does not
#       itself enforce — is DELIVERED AT THE MOMENT IT MUST TAKE EFFECT. Prose
#       is not a destination; it is a FAILURE TO LOCATE A DELIVERY MOMENT, and
#       where it remains it is labelled as such.
#
#     THE FRAME THIS REPAIRS. The authoring rule quoted at the top of this
#     diagram has TWO branches and is total over its two answers. It has NO
#     PROSE BRANCH. TIER 3 was invented here, unauthorised by the rule this
#     diagram opens with, and most of the `EN-2` (b) answer was spent
#     legitimising what lands in it. D13 supplies the missing clause.
#
#     THREE COROLLARIES, and they do the work below:
#       (1) DRY IS THE WRONG MODEL for agent instruction. Its premise — stated
#           once, referenced perfectly thereafter — is false for agents. So
#           "something with more authority already says this" warrants a
#           deletion ONLY where that authority fires WHERE AND WHEN the
#           obligation bites.
#       (2) MULTI-HOOK IS A REASON TO DELIVER REPEATEDLY, not to stop. An
#           obligation firing at several moments is hung at every one of them.
#       (3) GENERAL VIGILANCE IS THE WEAKEST FORM. "Be on the lookout for X" is
#           worth little; "apply this lens to this artefact, now" is worth a
#           great deal. Diagram A's own vocabulary names the weakness
#           (`vigilance — a standing be-on-the-lookout with NO trigger point`)
#           without drawing the conclusion.
#
#     WHAT IT CHANGES HERE: D10 is REVERSED (selector recording is delivered on
#     edges 3 and 4). D8 SURVIVES but its warrant narrows from "legitimately
#     kept" to "DEFERRED, known-weak". D11's two failed deletions become steps,
#     and its two "kept" items turn out to have a delivery moment nobody had
#     asked about. TIER 3 keeps its place on the axis but becomes a RESIDUE
#     REGISTER, each item carrying WHY no moment was found — two items under
#     D13, then ONE once D14 rehomed :111-113.
#
#     WHAT IT DOES NOT CHANGE: which assets may be overridden. That is DEC-102,
#     on a different axis, and D13 narrows rather than contradicts it — an asset
#     can be overridable craft and still owe a delivery moment.
#
#     NOT DELIVERED BY THIS SLICE: the third clause landing in the shipped
#     authoring rule (exploring.toml:8-13) and propagating to every runbook
#     authored after it. Out of PHASE-08 scope; IMP-374 carries it.
#
#     >>> RV-325 F-2 — D13 AS RECORDED IS UNBOUNDED. It mandates delivery at
#         every firing moment and states NO admission limit, merge rule,
#         optionality rule or cost threshold. Applied literally, edge 3
#         accretes the 15 content items plus :197, :198+:200-201, :199, :203
#         and :144-161 — NINETEEN OR MORE required obligations on one edge.
#         And the mechanism makes that SERIAL: Mode::Sequence is "a cursor,
#         one step at a time, no skipping" (runbook.rs:201-205), and
#         `discharge` on a submission is an OPTION, NOT A VEC, because "a
#         batch of two would need an order Batch explicitly refuses to carry"
#         (submission.rs:636-641). Nineteen obligations = nineteen serial CAS
#         mutation turns before advance. D7's "costs almost nothing" was
#         asserted without evidence against this slice's OWN named risk R1,
#         "protocol ceremony exceeds its value" (design.md:568-572) — and
#         PHASE-09 EX-3 measures it only AFTER PHASE-08 builds the shape
#         (its EN-1 requires PHASE-08 complete). That is a post-mortem, not
#         a bound.
#
#         FIRST PROPOSED REMEDY — WRONG, recorded because the correction is
#         instructive. It read: obligations sharing a MOMENT and a SUBJECT
#         merge into ONE step carrying the coverage list. A GRANULARITY bound,
#         on the premise that D13 governs *when* and had been misread as also
#         governing *how many*. Half right — D13 does govern only *when* — but
#         merging 19 steps into 6 leaves the content in the WRONG ASSET KIND,
#         and arithmetic was doing the work a missing distinction should do.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# D14 — THE ADMISSION BOUND IS A DESTINATION RULE, NOT A COUNTING RULE.
#       Owner ruling, 2026-08-01, in response to RV-325 F-2. THE LARGEST
#       STRUCTURAL CHANGE IN THIS DIAGRAM.
#
#         "A set of heuristics to apply while doing the work is NOT a
#          checklist to tick off. It is FRAMING FOR THE ACTIVITY, and an exit
#          gate delivers it when the damage is done."
#
#     TIER 2 WAS ALWAYS TWO THINGS, AND THE CODE SAYS SO. runbook.rs imports
#     STORE from prompt.rs because "a runbook is the structured sibling of the
#     prose it guards, IN THE SAME STORE, so spelling the directory twice
#     would be two sources for one address" (runbook.rs:20-24).
#
#       2a RUNBOOK STEP (*.toml)      2b STAGE FRAGMENT (*.md)
#       selected by an EDGE           selected by a STAGE
#         boundary_runbook(from,to)     Fragment::for_stage (prompt.rs:107-115)
#       delivered ONCE, at the        delivered EVERY TURN IN THE STAGE,
#         advance attempt               re-emitted unless the agent holds an
#                                       exact name@digest receipt (DEC-078)
#       DISCHARGED — cursor, CAS      NOT discharged — it is framing
#       may say `verified` IFF a      n/a
#         verifier exits zero
#
#     THE DISCRIMINATOR IS COMPLETABILITY AT A BOUNDARY. Sort by:
#
#         CAN THIS BE TRUTHFULLY COMPLETED AT A SPECIFIC BOUNDARY, WITH A
#         FINITE RESULT WHOSE COMPLETION IS NECESSARY BEFORE ADVANCING?
#
#       · yes — a finite, gate-worthy act -> 2a  (selectors; scope reconcile)
#       · no  — a continuous lens with no -> 2b  (consider data-flow
#               truthful completion point        boundaries; don't hide
#                                                assumptions in polish)
#
#     VERIFICATION STRENGTH IS AN ORTHOGONAL AXIS, NOT THE SORTING RULE.
#     DischargeOutcome has three arms in the record but TWO ON THE WIRE: a
#     caller may say `attested` or `skipped` and MAY NEVER say `verified` —
#     "a step carrying no verifier cannot acquire the stronger word by any
#     route" (runbook.rs:604-606, 745-752). A verifier STRENGTHENS a step's
#     discharge from `attested` to `verified`; its availability does not
#     decide whether the obligation is a step. CODICIL: a 2a step shipping no
#     verifier must state why mandatory attestation earns blocking the edge.
