#     authorship the load path does not admit.
#     design.md §7 chose this store for a SOUND reason (KNOWN_STAGE_LABELS is
#     an ENFORCED lifecycle vocabulary — route/canon/preflight/slice/design/
#     inquisition/plan/phase-plan/execute/audit/reconcile/close — and
#     `drafting` is not a lifecycle stage). But it BUNDLED TWO SEPARABLE
#     QUESTIONS and answered one: (i) should intra-design obligations be
#     stage-band labels? NO, correctly. (ii) should they be project-
#     overridable? NEVER ASKED — inherited from the mechanism chosen for (i).
#     D14 makes this WORSE by moving more craft into the un-overridable
#     store. Owner ruling: BACKLOG the extension interface, and FLAG §7 as
#     likely needing reopening. Not resolved in PHASE-08.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE ON `VA-4` / `VH-1` (plan amended 2026-08-01, pre-start, pre-evidence).
# This diagram's finding that shrinkage-by-relocation defeats a byte-count check
# fed straight back into the plan: `VA-4`'s judgement clause was withdrawn to a
# new `VH-1` (the owner reads the prose and judges), and `VA-4` was strengthened
# into a DISPOSITION TABLE that must account for every part of the incumbent
# body with runbook bytes stated SEPARATELY. Reviewers: that table is the
# artefact to attack, and this diagram is what it must be consistent with.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

direction: down

classes: {
  stage: {
    shape: rectangle
    style: { stroke-width: 3; fill: "#eef4fb"; stroke: "#2b6cb0"; font-size: 22 }
  }
  terminal: {
    shape: rectangle
    style: { stroke-width: 4; fill: "#e6fffa"; stroke: "#234e52"; font-size: 22; multiple: true }
  }
  engine: {
    shape: hexagon
    style: { fill: "#fffaf0"; stroke: "#b7791f"; stroke-width: 3; font-size: 13 }
  }
  shipped: {
    shape: document
    style: { fill: "#f0fff4"; stroke: "#276749"; stroke-width: 3; font-size: 13 }
  }
  proposed: {
    shape: document
    style: { fill: "#f0fff4"; stroke: "#276749"; stroke-dash: 4; font-size: 13 }
  }
  prose: {
    shape: parallelogram
    style: { fill: "#f7fafc"; stroke: "#718096"; stroke-dash: 4; font-size: 13 }
  }
  human: {
    shape: diamond
    style: { fill: "#f0fff4"; stroke: "#276749"; font-size: 13 }
  }
  open: {
    shape: callout
    style: { fill: "#fff5f5"; stroke: "#c53030"; stroke-width: 2; font-size: 14 }
  }
  decided: {
    shape: callout
    style: { fill: "#f0fff4"; stroke: "#276749"; stroke-width: 2; font-size: 14 }
  }
  note: {
    shape: text
    style: { font-size: 13; italic: true }
  }
}

legend: "Legend — the mediation axis" {
  near: top-left
  style: { fill: "#ffffff"; stroke: "#a0aec0" }
  l1: "TIER 1 · engine invariant\nRust, closed, unoverridable" { class: engine }
  l2: "TIER 2a · runbook step — SHIPPED\nedge-keyed, sequenced, dischargeable" { class: shipped }
  l3: "TIER 2a · runbook step — PROPOSED\nnot yet built" { class: proposed }
  l3b: "TIER 2b · stage fragment (*.md) — D14\nstage-keyed, every turn, NOT dischargeable" { class: proposed }
  l4: "TIER 3 · residue register\nno delivery moment located" { class: prose }
  l5: "waits on the human\n(still a TIER 1 condition)" { class: human }
  l6: "OPEN — needs a ruling" { class: open }
  l7: "RULED ON — 2026-08-01" { class: decided }
}

# ===========================================================================
# THE SPINE — five stages, four forward edges.
# Each edge container holds its TIER 1 conditions and its TIER 2 runbook.
# Conditions are quoted from `gate::boundary_conditions` (gate.rs:161-185);
# runbooks from `gate::boundary_runbook` (gate.rs:199-204).
# ===========================================================================

s_exp: "Stage::Exploring" { class: stage }
s_inq: "Stage::Inquiring" { class: stage }
s_dra: "Stage::Drafting" { class: stage }
s_rev: "Stage::Reviewing" { class: stage }
s_loc: "Stage::Locked\n(terminal — for_stage yields None)" { class: terminal }

# --- EDGE 1 -----------------------------------------------------------------
e1: "EDGE · exploring → inquiring" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; fill: "#fcfdfe" }
  c1: "GoverningContextRecorded" { class: engine }
  c2: "InitialConcernsRecorded" { class: engine }
  rb: "RUNBOOK exploring.toml — SHIPPED\nmode = sequence · 5 steps · all required" { class: shipped }
  st: "explore.scope\nexplore.research  ← verified: doctrine verify research-current\nexplore.canon\nexplore.memory\nexplore.triage" { class: shipped }
  n: "The ONLY populated row in boundary_runbook today.\nPHASE-16 shipped it; it is the exemplar every\nproposal below is measured against." { class: note }
  rb -> st
}

# --- EDGE 2 -----------------------------------------------------------------
e2: "EDGE · inquiring → drafting" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; fill: "#fcfdfe" }
  c1: "BlockingInquiriesDispositioned" { class: engine }
  c2: "UserAcceptsSufficiency" { class: human }
  rb: "RUNBOOK inquiring.toml — SETTLED (F-5)\nmode = sequence · 2 steps · both required\n· inquire.knowledge — record, via /knowledge, what THIS\n  INQUIRY settled that outlives the session. Subject is\n  this run's answers, NOT the design at large. Does NOT\n  enumerate the record kinds (see note)\n· inquire.scope — update OR EXPLICITLY CONFIRM the slice\n  scope against the decisions this inquiry accepted\n'or explicitly confirm' is what makes it finite; without\nit the step is unsatisfiable when nothing changed.\nNeither ships a verifier — warrants in thin-adapter (a.3)." { class: proposed }
  craft: "FRAGMENT design-prompts/inquiry.md (2b) — D14\ndelivered EVERY TURN IN **EXPLORING AND INQUIRING**\nBOTH — Fragment::for_stage maps Exploring|Inquiring to\nthe ONE Inquiry variant (prompt.rs:107-115). Corrected\nat RV-325 F-8; the diagram previously said Inquiring\nonly, which hid a second delivery moment from the\ndisposition. Wanted, not tolerated: 'Exploring and\ninquiring are both question-shaping work' (prompt.rs:109)\nand every item below holds in both. Splitting costs a\nnew variant, a 5th asset and duplicate prose.\nNOTE: Exploring therefore carries BOTH asset kinds at\nonce — exploring.toml (edge 1) and this fragment.\n· one question per message\n· 2-3 options with tradeoffs + a recommendation\n· focus on purpose / constraints / success criteria\n  / verification strategy\n· :98 prefer multiple-choice questions — THE record's\n  own central example of overridable craft\nTHIS is what makes DEC-102's 'craft moved out of skill\nprose into data' TRUE — and it does so WITHOUT D12." { class: proposed }
  n: "WHY THIS EDGE GETS THE CHEAPEST AND MOST IMPORTANT\nRUNBOOK. SKILL.md:98 'prefer multiple choice questions\nwhen possible' CONTRADICTS this project's own CLAUDE.md,\nwhich asks for prose forks in design loops. DEC-102\ncites exactly this as its live proof that craft is\ntaste, not doctrine.\n\nBut DEC-102's consequences ALREADY CLAIM 'the craft has\ngenuinely moved out of skill prose into data'. That is\nNOT YET TRUE: :98 is INQUIRY craft and PHASE-16 shipped\nthe EXPLORING runbook, a different edge. The record's\nown central example is still sitting unfixed in the\nfile. This runbook is what makes DEC-102 true rather\nthan aspirational — which is why it was added to the\ntwo the runbook sketch had assigned." { class: note }
  kn: "THE /knowledge STEP — an instance of this phase's\nwhole defect class. SKILL.md:107-109 names THREE kinds\n(QUE / DEC / ASM) of a closed SEVEN — assumption,\ndecision, question, constraint, evidence, hypothesis,\nconcept. A skill restating a Doctrine-owned closed\nvocabulary is a second source of truth that can\ndisagree, and this one already does.\n\nThe step says 'record it via /knowledge' and STOPS.\nLinking conventions need nothing either: /knowledge\nSKILL.md:42-43,60-62 already pins `link <REC> shapes\n<TARGET>`, `spawns` for work caused, `EVD supports|\ndisputes`, and 'the dependent work item authors the\nedge — records never author needs/after themselves'.\nRestating those here would recreate the same problem\none level up.\n\nNOT a duplicate of `BlockingInquiriesDispositioned`\nbeside it: `src/design_run/` writes NO knowledge\nrecords (every DEC- hit in it is a doc comment citing\none). Run inquiries are runtime state scoped to the\nrun; knowledge records are durable and outlive it.\nComplementary, not parallel." { class: note }
  rb -> craft
}

# --- EDGE 3 -----------------------------------------------------------------
e3: "EDGE · drafting → reviewing" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; fill: "#fcfdfe" }
  c1: "RequiredSectionsExist" { class: engine }
  c2: "MaterialisationCurrent" { class: engine }
  rb: "RUNBOOK · mode = sequence · ONE STEP (D14)\n· :144-161 record design-target selectors\n  (D10 reversed; also on edge 4)" { class: proposed }
  frag: "FRAGMENT design-prompts/drafting.md (2b) — D14\ndelivered EVERY DRAFTING TURN, not at this gate\n· the 15 content items as ONE LENS\n  (SKILL.md:118-143, diagram A s4.d3list)\n· :197 foundation sections first\n· :198 assumptions not hidden in polish\n· :199 altitude — design is not planning\n· :200-201 polish is not progress\n· :203 governance applied to choices\nshipped baseline is 15 lines; this is the bulk\nof what leaves the skill" { class: proposed }
  n: "D7/D14 — RULED, THEN REPLACED. These are the section-\nCONTENT obligations (boundaries, signatures, data flow,\ninvariants, C4, test titles, …). The conditions here\ncheck sections EXIST and are MATERIALISED — they say\nnothing about whether the content is any good.\n\nD7 filled that gap with 19 STEPS. RV-325 F-2 showed\nthat is 19 serial CAS turns, against this slice's own\nnamed risk R1. D14's answer is not a smaller number:\nthe 15 items are a QUALITY LENS, and a lens delivered\nat the exit arrives after the draft is written.\n\nONE step survives — the one obligation that COMPLETES\nAT THIS BOUNDARY and must, before advancing.\nThe DEC-101 'fake determinism' tension is GONE rather\nthan conceded: fragment prose has no order to fake." { class: note }
  rb -> n
  frag -> n
}

# --- EDGE 4 -----------------------------------------------------------------
e4: "EDGE · reviewing → locked" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; fill: "#fcfdfe" }
  c1: "SectionAttestationsCurrent" { class: engine }
  c2: "IntegratedReviewPresent" { class: engine }
  c3: "BlockingFindingsDisposed" { class: engine }
  c4: "UserAcceptanceAttested" { class: human }
  rb: "RUNBOOK · mode = sequence · the DISCRETE ACTS (D14)\n· :202 merged with :115-116 — reconcile slice scope\n· :144-161 record design-target selectors (2nd hook)\n· :187-188 first clause — repeat passes (RV-325 F-1)" { class: proposed }
  frag: "FRAGMENT design-prompts/reviewing.md (2b) — D14\ndelivered EVERY REVIEWING TURN\n· the attack surfaces: vague sections, hidden\n  assumptions, weak verification, missing\n  code-impact detail (SKILL.md:162-191,\n  diagram A s6.a1/a2)\n· :203 governance applied — SECOND COPY, verbatim.\n  Owner ruled the duplication acceptable; the DRY\n  instinct that would object here is the instinct\n  D13 rejects" { class: proposed }
  n: "D7 — RULED, THEN SPLIT BY D14. Four conditions\nalready: the heaviest edge. The conditions check a\nreview is PRESENT and its findings DISPOSED; they say\nnothing about whether the sweep was any good.\n\nThe attack surfaces are a LENS SET — the same ruling\nas the 15 content items on edge 3 — so they are\nframing for reviewing, not a checklist at its exit.\nWhat stays as steps is what COMPLETES AT THE BOUNDARY." { class: note }
  rb -> n
  frag -> n
}

s_exp -> e1 -> s_inq
s_inq -> e2 -> s_dra
s_dra -> e3 -> s_rev
s_rev -> e4 -> s_loc

# Backward motion. DEC-067: regressing returns the run to the edge's origin,
# where it faces the guard again. The incumbent's five "back" edges out of
# Adversarial review (A: s6 -> s2/s3/s4) are this, and they need no special
# modelling — a regression is not a distinct transition vocabulary.
s_rev -> s_exp: "regression — re-faces every\nguard on the way back up\n(DEC-067)" {
  style: { stroke: "#805ad5"; stroke-dash: 3 }
}

# ===========================================================================
# ORTHOGONAL REGIONS — D4. These run BESIDE the stage machine, not inside it.
# Evidence: DEC-065; diagram B `runstate.models`.
# ===========================================================================

orth: "Orthogonal state models — NOT sub-states (DEC-065)" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; fill: "#fbfcfe" }
  m1: "inquiry lifecycle" { class: engine }
  m2: "traversal — cursor + posture\n(user-lockable; refusal at refusal.rs:52)" { class: engine }
  m3: "attestation / review standing" { class: engine }
  m4: "delegation — selects delegation.md\nby DELEGATION state, not by stage" { class: engine }
  m5: "recovery / resume" { class: engine }
  m6: "runbook standing — cursor + discharges\nPHASE-16; third derived input to gate::advance" { class: shipped }
  m7: "revision — every mutation CAS or refuse" { class: engine }
  n: "The incumbent skill flattens all seven of these into one\nnumbered list. That is the single biggest reason its prose\nreads as a state machine but does not behave like one." { class: note }
}

orth -> s_dra: "consulted by the guards,\nadvance independently" {
  style: { stroke: "#4a5568"; stroke-dash: 2 }
}

# ===========================================================================
# THE MAPPING PANEL — D1. Eight prose states onto five engine stages.
# This IS the line-by-line disposition `EN-2(a)` asks for, in table form.
# ===========================================================================

mapping: "D1 · the eight prose states, disposed" {
  near: top-right
  style: { fill: "#ffffff"; stroke: "#2b6cb0"; stroke-width: 2 }
  t: |||md
    | # | incumbent prose state | target | tier |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | 1 | Explore context | `Stage::Exploring` + edge-1 runbook | 1+2 — **moved, shipped** |
    | 2 | Ask clarifying questions | `Stage::Inquiring` + edge-2 runbook | 1 + 2 — **ruled, D12** |
    | 3 | Propose 2-3 approaches | **not a state** — craft inside Inquiring | 2 — folded into the edge-2 runbook (**D12**) |
    | 4 | Present design | `Stage::Drafting` | 1 + proposed edge-3 runbook |
    | 5 | Write design.md | **not a state** — an effect (D2) | 1 — `MaterialisationCurrent` |
    | 6 | Adversarial review | `Stage::Reviewing` | 1 + proposed edge-4 runbook |
    | 7 | Integrate review feedback | **not a state** — work in Reviewing (D3) | 1 — two exit conditions |
    | 8 | Transition to planning (`:26-28`, XML `:54-56`) | terminal exit — retained as adapter prose | 3 — **ruled, D8** |
  |||
}

# ===========================================================================
# THE RESIDUE — what has NO edge to hang on. This is where TIER 3 is decided,
# and it is the direct source of `EN-2` answers (b) "what the skill still owns
# on activation" and (c) "what it owns on recovery".
# ===========================================================================

residue: "The residue — obligations with no natural edge" {
  style: { stroke: "#c53030"; fill: "#fffbfb" }

  guards: "D9/D11 · the eight guardrails, TRIAGED (SKILL.md:192-204)" {
    class: decided
    g: "RE-TRIAGED TWICE. D13 sorted by WHEN MUST THIS ARRIVE;\nD14 then asked WHAT KIND OF THING IS IT.\nOutcome 1 / 1 / 5 / 1 / 1 over 9 rows\n(D13: 1 / 1 / 6 / 1. First pass: 4 / 1 / 2 / 3.)\n\nDELETE — the authority fires WHERE IT BITES (1):\n  :196 -> boot snapshot, in context every turn\n  (A s6.a6 was a second row here — REMOVED, RV-325 F-1:\n   double-disposed inside :162-191, and its evidence\n   covered only the no-presumed-approval clause. The\n   repeat-pass clause becomes an EDGE-4 STEP.)\nTO THE SEALED HYMN (1):\n  :194 'the design doc is canon for design intent'\nTO A STAGE FRAGMENT — 2b, a LENS not an act (5):\n  :197 :198 :199 :200-201 -> drafting.md\n  :203 -> drafting.md AND reviewing.md (duplication\n         ruled acceptable by the owner)\nTO AN EDGE AS A STEP — 2a, a discrete act (1):\n  :202 -> edge 4, merging with :115-116\nTO THE BOOT SNAPSHOT — doctrine-wide (1):\n  :111-113 qualify doc-local acronyms. NOT residue and\n  NOT design-scoped; the missing second clause of\n  routing-process.md:60-63. Edit carried as backlog.\n\nTHE `step` COLUMN COLLAPSED FROM 6 TO 1. Five were\nlenses, not acts. That is the single largest correction\nin this revision, and why F-2's ceremony objection\nDISSOLVES rather than being traded away."
  }
  gn: "Diagram A classed these `vigilance` — standing, no trigger point.\nThat is precisely what makes them hard: a runbook step needs an\nEDGE, and 'be on the lookout' has none. RULED: triage into three\nbuckets, which are not exclusive —\n  (i)  the ones that are really INVARIANTS restate what the engine\n       already enforces -> delete, or move to the sealed stage hymn;\n  (ii) the ones that are really STEPS at a specific moment -> assign\n       an edge and make them runbook steps;\n  (iii) the rest are genuinely ambient judgement -> TIER 3, and the\n       adapter legitimately keeps them.\nThe owner's ruling adds the part that matters: this triage is a\nDESIGN ACTIVITY, not a mechanical sort, and it IS the `EN-2` (b)\nanswer. Do not expect it to fall out of a table." { class: note }

  sel: "D10 · design-target selector recording (SKILL.md:144-161)" {
    class: decided
    s: "`doctrine slice selector add <id> … --intent design-target`\nhas a command but NO design-run hook and no prompt (diagram B gap g4).\nDiagram A classed it an ORPHAN: prose process with no state at all.\nThe prose says 'when the code-impact section locks' — edge-shaped,\npointing at edge 3.\n\nRULED 'stays as prose' (one step on one edge would be a worse lie,\nasserting a completeness the obligation lacks) — then REVERSED BY\nD13. Unpicked, that objection has two parts: OVERCLAIMING is a real\nharm; MULTI-HOOK is not, and under D13 it argues the other way.\n\nNOW: STEPS ON EDGES 3 AND 4, with text that does not overclaim —\n'record the design-target selectors the code-impact section NOW\nCOMMITS TO' is true at each firing, final at neither.\n\nRESIDUAL, open: hooks ACROSS DESIGN AND PLAN stay unmodelled —\n/plan has no runbook machinery yet. A known coverage gap now,\nnot a warrant for delivering nothing where we can."
  }

  exit: "D8 · the Locked exit (SKILL.md:26-28, XML :54-56)" {
    class: decided
    x: "CITATION CORRECTED: an earlier revision cited :157-162. State 8 is NOT\nthere — :144-161 is the SELECTOR section (D10) and :162 is the\n'### Adversarial review' heading. There is no '### Transition to planning'\nbody section at all; state 8 exists only as the numbered-list entry at\n:26-28 and the XML state at :54-56. Not cosmetic: :26-28 falls inside\n:15-58, which the size arithmetic marked DELETED OUTRIGHT — so the very\nlines this ruling RETAINS were double-disposed, inside the table `VA-4`\nwas amended to require accounting for every part of the body.\n\n`for_stage` yields None at Locked BY DESIGN, but there is still\nwork to instruct: record the lifecycle move (`doctrine slice status\n<id> plan`) then invoke `/plan` (diagram B gap g2).\n\nRULED: (a) — the adapter KEEPS IT as TIER 3 prose. Rejected: (b) a\nterminal runbook keyed to Locked, which breaks D5's edge-keying since\nLocked has no outbound forward edge; and (c) a conclude/handoff\noutput on `doctrine design`.\n\nThe reasoning is forward-looking rather than minimal: if this\nprogramme succeeds, `/plan` receives the same treatment, and the\nstage-to-stage handoff interface is better designed ONCE against two\nreal cases than guessed at now against one."
  }
}

# ===========================================================================
# WHAT THE ADAPTER STILL OWNS — the EN-2 (b) and (c) answers. Q1-Q4 all landed
# on 2026-08-01 as D7-D10, so nothing here is provisional on their account any
# more. Kept in the diagram so the sketch back-solves from here.
# ===========================================================================

adapter: "The thin adapter — what is left for the skill" {
  style: { stroke: "#718096"; fill: "#f7fafc"; stroke-width: 2 }
  act: "ON ACTIVATION\n· establish or resume the run (`doctrine design start|resume`)\n· surface the envelope and DO WHAT IT SAYS\n· nothing about workflow order — the machine owns that" { class: prose }
  keeps: "THE RESIDUE REGISTER (EN-2 b) — under D13 this is no longer\na list of things KEPT, it is a list of things NOT DELIVERED, each\ncarrying WHY. ONE ITEM:\n\n· :26-28 the Locked-exit instructions (D8)\n  WHY: DEFERRED, not ambient. The moment is precise — the instant\n  the run reaches terminal. Stage::Locked has NO outbound forward\n  edge to key a runbook to, and Fragment::for_stage yields None\n  there, so NEITHER 2a NOR 2b can hold it as the machine stands.\n  The mechanism is declined until /plan gives a second case to\n  design the handoff interface against.\n\nSHRANK THREE TIMES, each contraction from a better question.\nFIRST: three items, arguing their smallness was the claim.\nD13 asked WHEN MUST THIS ARRIVE — :199 and :200-201 answered\nimmediately and left. Two.\nD14 asked WHAT KIND OF THING IS IT — :111-113 turned out to be\nneither design-scoped nor unenforceable, just badly homed. One.\n\nONE IS A DEFENSIBLE FLOOR. :26-28 is not residue because no\nmoment exists — its moment is the most precisely identifiable in\nthe file. It is residue because building a handoff interface from\na single case is how you get an interface shaped by one example.\nThat is a deferral WITH A NAMED CONDITION FOR REPAYMENT, which is\na different sentence from 'we could not place this' — and telling\nthose two apart is exactly what VH-1 exists to judge." { class: prose }
  rec: "ON RECOVERY\n· no run exists -> start one\n· a stale run exists -> `resume` with --known-revision/--known-fragment\n· D5 means re-entry needs no special casing per state: the run\n  reports where it stands and which obligations are outstanding" { class: prose }
  deg: "ON ABSENCE OR ERROR (EN-2 e)\n· `doctrine design` missing or erroring -> SURFACE IT, do not\n  self-heal and do not fall back to executing the old machine\n· skills ship from github:davidlee/doctrine@main on a manual\n  plugin update, so skill and binary CAN desync in BOTH directions\n· with a known user population of ~4, detection beats degradation\n  engineering" { class: prose }
  n: "TARGET SIZE (EN-2 d) — the incumbent is 214 lines / 10,178 bytes.\nThis diagram found that a byte-count check is defeatable: under\nDEC-101 the checklists move into runbook TOML, a DIFFERENT FILE, so\nshrinkage by relocation is trivially achievable and no agent reading\na total can tell it from shrinkage by mechanism.\n\nRESOLVED IN THE PLAN, 2026-08-01: `VA-4`'s judgement clause was\nwithdrawn to a new `VH-1` (the owner reads the prose and judges,\nbounded by D7/D8/D10 as the decisions that make residue legitimate),\nand `VA-4` now requires a DISPOSITION TABLE accounting for every part\nof the incumbent body, runbook bytes stated SEPARATELY rather than\nnetted. So (d) answers a target for the SKILL body alone, and the\ntable carries the rest." { class: note }
}

# ===========================================================================
# FOR THE REVIEWER — what to attack.
# ===========================================================================

review: "For the reviewer" {
  near: bottom-left
  style: { fill: "#ffffff"; stroke: "#c53030"; stroke-width: 2 }
  r: |||md
    **RE-RANKED after a self-check pass, 2026-08-01.** D5 was item 1 as the
    claim most likely to be false; checking it against source showed it is the
    best-evidenced claim here, and it is now item 5 restated as a durability
    question. The D11 triage was item 6 and is now item 1, because the same pass
    found two of its four deletions do not hold. Both movements are disclosed
    rather than quietly re-ordered — a ranking that was wrong is itself evidence
    about the method that produced it.

    1. **D13 / DEC-103 is new, it reshaped this diagram, and it is the thing to
       attack first.** *Instruction is delivered at the moment it must take
       effect; prose is a failure to locate a delivery moment, not a
       destination.* Ruled after D1-D12; it reversed D10, re-warranted D8,
       repaired two failed deletions and collapsed TIER 3 from three items to
       two. Three attacks are open, and they land on the RULING, not the phase:
       - **Is it too strong?** It licenses hanging an obligation on EVERY edge
         where it fires. Followed far enough each runbook accretes every
         standing rule and the steps become noise an agent scrolls past — the
         incumbent's existing failure mode relocated into TOML. Where is the
         limit, and does the edge-3 runbook (fifteen content items PLUS four
         moved obligations) already cross it? Strongest attack available.
       - **Is :111-113 genuinely unenforceable?** The whole "we found no
         mechanism" claim rests on that one item. The stage hymn IS rendered
         every turn — if a per-message obligation can ride it, the residue is
         one item, not two.
       - **Does it pull against DEC-101?** "Imposing an order on a coverage set
         is fake determinism" and "deliver at the point of effect" strain
         against each other when many obligations share one moment. Edge 3 is
         where that tension is now thickest.
    2. **DEC-102's overclaim is specific, not rhetorical.** Its consequences do
       not merely say the craft "has moved into data"; they say the SKILL.md:98
       / CLAUDE.md contradiction "is therefore fixed FOR THIS REPO". :98 is
       verbatim unchanged. And DEC-102's own `alternatives` field rejects the
       keep-everything-fixed option BECAUSE it "leaves the SKILL.md:98
       contradiction unfixable in place" — so amending the record withdraws a
       consequence its rejection reasoning leans on. D12 is the cheaper repair
       of the two once the record is read in full. Still worth attacking from
       the other side; no longer the balanced fork earlier drafts described.
    3. **Both new runbooks are coverage sets shipping as sequences (D7).** This
       was ruled deliberately, not overlooked: forcing an arbitrary order on a
       short coverage set costs the agent almost nothing, and "stop agents
       forgetting" is the actual purpose. DEC-101's "imposing an order on a
       coverage set is fake determinism" is conceded as true and judged cheap.
       If you think the order will actually mislead — that an agent will read
       sequence position as dependency and do worse work — that is the attack
       to make, and it lands on D7 rather than on IMP-373.
    4. **Re-entry is asserted, not proven.** EN-2(f) asks what breaks in the
       eight skills that cross-reference `/design`. Every hit is a routing
       reference, not an internals reference, so the claim is that resumption
       accounts for all of them. That claim is untested here deliberately —
       it is offered to the reviewer as the thing most worth probing.
    5. **D5 is durability-limited, not doubtful.** Settled by gate.rs:150-158
       (a nine-line literal `matches!`) and locked by an exhaustive 25-pair test
       (tests.rs:56-77) — a fifth edge cannot land silently. Do not spend a turn
       re-deriving it. The residual worth probing: IS A SECOND FORWARD EDGE EVER
       WANTED? If design §5.4 or DEC-067 contemplates a branch, an optional
       stage or a skip, D5 is true-but-temporary and every disposition here is
       scheduled to move. Blast radius is three call sites, not one — see the
       evidence note under D5.
    6. **The residue may still be larger than it looks.** The guardrail census
       is now stated honestly (D11), but diagram A also shows `vigilance` nodes
       scattered INSIDE states (A s2.rules, A s3.note). Only A s6.a6 was pulled
       in. A sweep of A for every `vigilance` and `orphan` node is the honest
       denominator for TIER 3, and it has not been run.
    7. **Method: what the six questions did not name.** `Outcomes` (:205-214)
       was triaged only when the size arithmetic refused to close, because no
       EN-2 question named it. The self-check pass is a second instance: state
       8's line range, D5's circular citation and three conflicting guardrail
       counts were all in material no question interrogated. Ask what else is
       unnamed.
    8. **B is stale and this diagram inherits from it.** Diagram B predates
       PHASE-16: "six writer acts" is now seven, gap g1 is closed, gap g3 is
       partly closed. If anything here reads as contradicting B, check whether
       B is simply older before treating it as a conflict.
